-- Tests of ie-schedule-definitions.l4: the defined-term tables for Schedule Parts 1 and 2, the functions that apply the entries, and s 21(1), (2).
-- Part A (the table) is checked entry by entry. For each of the 40 entries, the word, the part, whether the entry says MEANS, INCLUDES or DOES NOT INCLUDE,
-- and the case or context are derived by pattern from the raw text of that entry's own line, which is quoted beside the assertions.
-- The number of things the entry names, and the first (and where there are several, the last) of them, were read off the line by hand.
-- Part B applies the entries. Expected values come from the text of the entry and are written beside each assertion.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ie-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `ie-general`
IMPORT `ie-operation-and-construction`
IMPORT `ie-schedule-definitions`

§ `Part A: the table, entry by entry`


-- line 217 of the text: “affidavit”, in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to declare instead of swearin
#ASSERT (`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `the word defined` EQUALS "affidavit"
#ASSERT (`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `the part of the Schedule` EQUALS 1
#ASSERT (`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `how the entry gives the meaning` EQUALS `the entry says it includes`
#ASSERT (`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `the case or context in which the entry applies` EQUALS JUST "in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to declare instead of swearing"
#ASSERT count ((`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `the things the entry names`) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT elem "declaration" ((`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `the things the entry names`)
#ASSERT elem "declaration" ((`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `the things the entry names`)
#ASSERT count ((`the Part 1 entry for` `affidavit`)'s `what the entry points at`) EQUALS 0

